  "articleBody": "When it comes to your roof, flashing is one of those unsung heroes that often gets overlooked until there's a problem. There are a lot of myths floating around about flashing, and buying into them can lead to costly water damage and bigger headaches down the road. Especially in Texas, where intense weather can really test these critical components, understanding the facts is key to protecting your home.\n\nMyth: You can fix any flashing issue with more caulk. Fact: This is a huge misconception. Caulk is a temporary sealant, not a substitute for properly installed or repaired flashing. Relying on caulk alone is a common mistake that leads to leaks because it cracks, shrinks, and deteriorates over time, leaving gaps for water to enter. Flashing needs to be a continuous water-shedding detail that physically directs water away from vulnerable areas, not just a surface seal. When caulk is used as a primary fix, it's often delaying an inevitable, larger repair.\n\nMyth: If your roof isn't leaking, your flashing must be fine. Fact: Not necessarily! Flashing can be damaged or improperly installed without immediately causing a visible leak inside your home. Damage like corrosion, looseness, or separation from vertical surfaces might not show up as an interior stain right away, but it's creating a pathway for water intrusion during heavy rains or wind-driven moisture. By the time you see a leak, the damage could be more extensive than it would have been if caught earlier during a routine inspection.\n\nMyth: Flashing only needs to be checked when the roof is replaced. Fact: Flashing should be included in routine roof inspections at least twice a year, typically in spring and fall, and definitely after any severe weather. It's a critical component that takes a lot of abuse, especially around chimneys, skylights, and vents. These areas are vulnerable points where water can easily penetrate if the flashing isn't in top condition. Ignoring it between roof replacements is a gamble you don't want to take.\n\nMyth: All flashing is the same, so any repair will do. Fact: Flashing materials and installation methods need to be compatible with your roof system and local building codes. Improper materials or installation can void warranties or, worse, fail quickly. FEMA even specifically recommends flashing that meets or exceeds local code requirements in high-wind areas, showing how important specific details are. You need materials that can stand up to Texas conditions.\n\nWhat Can Change the Right Choice\nThe best approach to flashing issues depends on the type of damage, the age of your roof, and local building requirements. Sometimes a repair is sufficient, while other times, replacement is the safer, more durable option.\n\nWhat to Do Next\nIf you suspect you have flashing issues or it’s been a while since a professional assessment, contact Discounted Roofing Co. We can provide an honest assessment of your flashing's condition and recommend the best course of action.",
  "datePublished": "2026-10-03"
}